Security through obscurity helps no one -- people just sit around
wondering what's wrong instead of doing something about it, while
others take the necessary 30 seconds to spot the secret in the man
page (like I just did).  Besides, it's real easy to fix.

Gzip can be spoofed through the environment, which nethack passes
untouched.

If you don't have the source code (or don't want to recompile it),
compile the following program as "gszp" and put it in the same
directory as gzip.  Then patch the nethack binary to change "gzip" to
"gszp".  (Don't forget the changing the binary clears the set-id
bits.)

----- gszp.c
static char *envinit[1];
char gzip[]     = "/local/bin/gzip";  /* Change this as needed */

main (int argc, char **argv)
{
    if (argc)
	argv[0] = gzip;

    execve(gzip, argv, envinit);
}
-----

To fix the source, just change the execv in the compress code in
files.c to execve, and add the variable envinit.


